-  private final io.netty.util.internal.AppendableCharSequence seq;
-  private final int maxLength;
-  private int size;
-  io.netty.handler.codec.http.HttpObjectDecoder$HeaderParser(io.netty.util.internal.AppendableCharSequence, int);
-  public io.netty.util.internal.AppendableCharSequence parse(io.netty.buffer.ByteBuf);
-  public void reset();
-  public boolean process(byte) throws java.lang.Exception;
-  protected io.netty.handler.codec.TooLongFrameException newException(int);
-}
```

# Supporting Evidence
- The diff shows complete removal of the `HeaderParser` class which was responsible for HTTP header processing
- The class implemented critical interfaces (`ByteBufProcessor`) and had methods for parsing, resetting, and processing bytes
- The trace keywords included `HttpObjectDecoder$HeaderParser` suggesting it was part of the execution path

# Open Questions
1. Was the functionality of `HeaderParser` moved elsewhere in the codebase or completely redesigned?
2. How does the removal affect HTTP header processing in the new version?
3. Are there any compensating changes in the parent `HttpObjectDecoder` class that handle this functionality differently?
